-- title:
--   A partial sum of the fifteen class products
-- statement:
--   `BunkbedTransferClasses` expresses the Wierman--Ziff difference polynomial of Hollom's hypergraph as a sum of fifteen products $(\mathtt{ck}\,k)(\mathtt{wk}\,k)$ of degree-$6$ polynomials. Verifying all fifteen in a single step exceeds the platform's compile budget, so the identity is checked in two halves; `wzDlo` records the first half, $\sum_{k < 8} (\mathtt{ck}\,k)(\mathtt{wk}\,k)$, in expanded form.
--
--   It is homogeneous of degree $12$ in the four state probabilities $x = P_{abc}$, $y = P_{a|b|c}$, $z = P_{a|bc}$, $u = P_{ab|c} = P_{ac|b}$, with $347$ monomials. The split point $k = 8$ was chosen to balance the work: the two halves involve $23180$ and $20346$ monomial multiplications respectively.
-- source:
--   Gladkov, Pak, Zimin, *The bunkbed conjecture is false*, PNAS 122 (2025) no. 24, e2420725122; Section 3, Lemma 3.3.

import Definitions.Def_BunkbedTransferClasses

/-!
# A partial sum of the fifteen class products

`BunkbedTransferClasses` expresses the Wierman--Ziff difference polynomial as a sum of fifteen
products `ck k * wk k`. Verifying all fifteen in one step costs more than the platform's compile
budget allows, so the sum is checked in two halves, and `wzDlo` records the first half
`Σ_{k < 8} ck k * wk k` in expanded form: homogeneous of degree 12 in the Wierman--Ziff state
probabilities, with 347 monomials.

It is written as a sum of short `_p` pieces because a single long chain of monomial summands
elaborates superlinearly.
-/

set_option autoImplicit false
set_option linter.unusedVariables false

namespace Bunkbed.TM

open Bunkbed.Hyper

